## Why This Is Asked
This prompts designing scalable, isolated analytics pipelines with per-tenant data lakes and strong governance.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant isolation across storage and compute
- Exactly-once ingestion with idempotency
- OpenTelemetry data normalization
- Per-tenant data marts in Athena
- Data masking and RBAC
- Lifecycle and cost controls

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node.js Lambda dedup skeleton
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const ddb = new AWS.DynamoDB.DocumentClient();

exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const {tenant_id, message_id, payload} = JSON.parse(event.body || '{}');
  const key = {TenantId: tenant_id, MessageId: message_id};
  await ddb.put({TableName:'Idempotency', Item: key, ConditionExpression:'attribute_not_exists(MessageId)'}).promise();
  // forward to S3 after normalization
  return {statusCode: 200, body: 'ok'};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle late-arriving data across tenants?
- How would you test tenant isolation and masking at scale?

## Why This Is Asked
Real-world multi-tenant telemetry with cross-region needs robust isolation and cost-aware governance. This question probes end-to-end design, fault tolerance, and testing strategy beyond basic pipelines.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant isolation, RBAC via Lake Formation
- Exactly-once semantics via idempotent writes and dedupe stores
- Schema evolution with Glue catalog and Parquet partitioning
- Late-arriving data, backfill, and DLQ handling
- Cross-region replication, OpenTelemetry tracing, dynamic sampling

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo dedupe check
function isDuplicate(event, cache){
  const key = event.tenant + '|' + event.recordId;
  if (cache.has(key)) return true;
  cache.add(key);
  return false;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate idempotency at scale and what metrics would you monitor?
- What cost-optimization strategies would you apply for multi-tenant data marts across regions?
